\f1\fs22 Kathy did not like cells.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 She\'92d already been confined to at least one.\
She especially did not like cells dressed up as counselling rooms. This one had beanbags and cushions. A bowl of mints was left on the table so the interview transcript could record that refreshments had been offered. The room wanted you to agree it was kind, and it knew you\'92d eventually stop disagreeing it wasn\'92t.\
Kermit the frog sat upon a rainbow on the back wall, paint flaking off both in scabs as though the wall was telling Kermit and the rainbow to go back to where they came from.\
The only honest things in it were the rainbow rejection, the one-way window in the wall near the door, and a padded chair bolted to the floor. It had enough anchor points for ropes, plastic ties and chains to haunt Houdini.\
In the viewing room, the High Elvis and his cabinet peered through the observation window, faces tight. Sarah stood by the High Elvis, one hand on her belly, one trying not to rest for support on the full-length bench under the window. Hayley flanked the High Elvis on the other side, leaning on the bench and crouching over a microphone and a control panel.\
The Sages were in the room with Kathy, because of course they were. If the day was going to turn weird, you wanted those weirdos closest to the action. Which suited the Sages just fine, especially Chilo, who stood near the table as if the table belonged to him. Solo wandered the perimeter, peering at bolts and corners with the delighted curiosity of a man who had never met a room he didn\'92t find fascinating. Kleo took the seat opposite the padded chair, in which a patient sat unrestrained (as required by the Sages, especially Chilo), legs crossed, hands clasped in his lap with religious neatness. He looked relaxed in a way that suggested he\'92d never once been trapped anywhere he didn\'92t want to be. He wore a robe the colour of a third-use teabag; his sideburns were trimmed down to something modest and deliberate.\
A tag on the folder said: 
\f2\b\fs18 CHANGE OF HABIT SERIES \'97 DR CARPENTER.
\f1\b0\fs22\
Dr Carpenter smiled at them with the serene certainty of a man who\'92d been given permission to stop thinking. \'93My brothers and sisters,\'94 he said warmly.\
Kathy knew that brand of warmth, more dosage than devotion.\
Dr Carpenter drew a long breath and began to sing, \'93How Great Thou Art\'94. Hayley, as the only non-Elvi and therefore safe pair of hands, flicked a switch less than two bars in, cutting audio in the viewing room.\
Hayley flicked another switch to activate the mic. \'93No singing. No humming,\'94 she said into it, then thumbed the switch back. She watched Dr Carpenter stop singing, but waited for Kathy, who turned and gave her a thumbs-up. Hayley exhaled and restored audio to the viewing room.\
Chilo cleared his throat with careful gentleness, as if about to poke a bear and then blame the bear for being rude.\
\'93I\'92ve heard the Word,\'94 Dr Carpenter said before Chilo could frame his first question. \'93It was placed in my heart through harmony.\'94\
Kathy\'92s mind flashed to Sarah\'92s SARA readouts: patterns like language. Meaning travelling through air.\
Kleo leaned forward a fraction. \'93And the Word was?\'94\
Dr Carpenter\'92s smile widened. \'93Praise be to\'97\'94\
\'93Right,\'94 Kathy cut in. \'93We\'92re not doing the praise thing in here.\'94\
Dr Carpenter regarded her with gentle pity, like she\'92d confessed to being left-handed, or an actual tax-payer.\
\'93You cannot suppress truth,\'94 he said.\
Chilo reached into a small bag\'97where had he got a bag?\'97and produced a battered portable speaker.\
Kathy stiffened.\
\'93Rock and roll therapy,\'94 Chilo said. \'93A simple counter-frequency. We begin with the basics.\'94\
He pressed play.\
A scratchy recording of \'93Grey Walls of Tyres\'94 (not one of Chanta\'92s best) rattled into the room at a volume that was meant to be harmless.\
Dr Carpenter listened, head tilted slightly.\
No hips. No footwork. No involuntary pelvis.\
He closed his eyes and smiled like a saint. \'93Devil music,\'94 he murmured.\
About a minute into the song, the High Elvis\'92s voice came through the intercom. \'93It\'92s not working.\'94\
Kleo tapped a fingernail lightly against his knee.\
Once the song had played, Chilo shut the speaker off. Silence pressed in.\
Dr Carpenter sighed, content. \'93Better.\'94\
\'93Okay,\'94 Kathy said. \'93Let\'92s try something that isn\'92t a nostalgia assault.\'94\
She pulled out her tablet and opened a calm science documentary featuring finches and beak adaptation. She angled the screen so Dr Carpenter could see.\
Dr Carpenter leaned forward, polite interest arranged on his face like a mask.\
\'93Amazing,\'94 he said. \'93The Lord\'92s design.\'94\
Kathy\'92s jaw tightened. \'93It\'92s evolution.\'94\
He nodded serenely. \'93The Lord\'92s evolution.\'94\
Solo made a small appreciative noise from the corner.\
\'93Okay, let\'92s take it up a notch,\'94 Kathy said. \'93Language.\'94\
Chilo nodded. \'93Allow me to try Latin,\'94 he said, and Kathy shrugged, 
\f3\i okay
\f1\i0 .\
Dr Carpenter\'92s eyes widened slightly, as if pleased by the word itself.\
Chilo spoke carefully. \'93
\f3\i Ego sum filius Dei.
\f1\i0 \'94\
Dr Carpenter\'92s chest rose and fell. His eyes grew wet. Then he repeated it with reverence, like he\'92d always known it. \'93
\f3\i Ego sum filius Dei
\f1\i0 .\'94\
Chilo recited a few more Latin phrases, some from Catholic liturgical texts, others from a Roman town planner\'92s journal of the 2nd century of the Capitalist Era, starting from the birth of a prophet who taught that it was good to feed most of the people on as little as possible.\
Dr Carpenter faithfully repeated the liturgy, but did not repeat anything of the Ancient Roman by-laws on sewage disposal.\
After a few minutes of this, the High Elvis\'92s voice crackled through the intercom. \'93Stop feeding him.\'94\
Chilo stopped, looking faintly disappointed.\
Kathy stared at Dr Carpenter and watched him sit, glowing with borrowed authority.\
Solo wandered closer, bright-eyed. \'93What about Sanskrit?\'94\
Kathy blinked. \'93Sorry?\'94\
Solo nodded, pleased with himself. \'93Ancient. Sacred. Authoritative. Meaning arrives in the body before it gets rationalised by the mind.\'94\
Kathy looked at Kleo. Kleo didn\'92t tell him no.\
\'93Very well,\'94 Chilo said.\
He took paper and a Sharpie from his bag\'97of course there was paper and a Sharpie in the bag\'97and wrote in a slow, deliberate hand. Old. Heavy. Serious. He turned the page for Dr Carpenter to read.\
