\f1\fs22 \'93
\f3\i Nothing but man are you
\f1\i0 ,\'94 Dr Carpenter said to Kleo.\
Kathy leaned in closer; the characters looked convincing until she looked twice and resolved the words, syllable by syllable, into something else entirely.\
\'93That\'92s not Sanskrit,\'94 she said. \'93That\'92s an English cypher in Sanskrit clothes.\'94\
Dr Carpenter beamed at her as if she\'92d complimented his robe. He drew breath\'97\
Hayley\'92s voice cracked through the intercom like a slap. \'93Do not let him sing.\'94\
Too late.\
Dr Carpenter began to hum.\
Soft. Tender. The kind of melody you wanted to believe was harmless.\
Kleo rose, very calmly, and placed his hand\'97lightly\'97on Dr Carpenter\'92s shoulder.\
The humming wavered.\
Kleo leaned in, voice low, and for the first time Kathy heard something in him that wasn\'92t humour or patience.\
\'93Listen to me,\'94 Kleo said. \'93You will stop now.\'94\
The humming faltered, then died.\
Dr Carpenter blinked, serene again, smiling as if forgiveness had just been administered.\
Kathy looked at Kleo, then at the other Sages, and felt the sick click of realisation. They weren\'92t trying to cure it. So what the tax were they doing \'97 taking notes?\
Kleo straightened and looked at the High Elvis through the glass.\
\'93We can remove a belief,\'94 he said. \'93But not without replacing it.\'94\
Not that Kleo could see, but on the other side of the glass, the High Elvis\'92s shoulders sagged just slightly.\
Sarah shifted her weight, one hand pressing low against her back.\
Kathy swallowed and forced the words out. \'93So what do you replace it with?\'94\
Kleo didn\'92t smile this time.\
He looked at Dr Carpenter the way a surgeon looked at a tumour: without hatred, without affection, only with a cool interest in whether it could be treated.\
He looked into the one-way glass, for the people he knew were behind it, assessing whether they would notice the room tilting.\
\'93Praise the Dawkins,\'94 Kleo said softly, like a test and a prayer at once. He met Dr Carpenter\'92s eyes, and let him believe he had nothing to hide.\
Dr Carpenter blinked. For a heartbeat his serenity slipped. His throat worked. His eyes sharpened as if, briefly, a person was trying to come back online.\
\'93Praise the Dawkins,\'94 Kleo said again, even softer than before.\
Then, very quietly, as if the words had been pulled out of him by a hook:  \'93Praise the Dawkins,\'94 Dr Carpenter repeated.\
Kathy went cold. Not because the religious Elvis said it\'97people said all kinds of things under pressure\'97but because of what happened to his face afterwards. The feverish glow didn\'92t return. The performance didn\'92t return.\
He sat there, breathing hard, looking suddenly\'85 anchored. Like he\'92d remembered gravity.\
Kathy\'92s mouth went dry. \'93That\'92s\'85\'94\
Kleo\'92s eyes stayed on Dr Carpenter. \'93Yes.\'94\
On the other side of the glass, the High Elvis didn\'92t move. Sarah\'92s breath caught, and she cupped the underside of her belly as if to steady the weight of it. Hayley\'92s fingers flexed once.\
Kathy swallowed. The only thought she could manage was the worst one: 
\f3\i If you can swap a hymn out for Dawkins, you can swap Dawkins out for anything.
\f1\i0\
Dr Carpenter lifted his head, and for the first time his voice sounded less like a sermon and more like a man trying to speak with his own mouth.\
\'93Praise\'85\'94 he began again, and stopped, as if startled by himself.\
Kathy forced air into her lungs. \'93Don\'92t,\'94 she said. \'93One repetition. That\'92s enough.\'94 She didn\'92t need to be around for the full entrainment.\
Kleo finally looked at her. His expression held no comfort at all.\
The High Elvis grunted, satisfied. \'93We can deploy this,\'94 he said.\
\'93Praise the Dawkins.\'94 Dr Carpenter smiled.
